<BaseLayout title="About the Book" description="About Teaching and Learning Mathematics Online and its editors, James P. Howard, II and John F. Beyers.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">About TLMO</p>
      <h1>Mathematics is hard enough before the Wi-Fi gets involved.</h1>
      <p><em>Teaching and Learning Mathematics Online</em> is a collection about the practical work of teaching mathematics and statistics when instructor and student are not necessarily in the same room—or even on the same schedule.</p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">The editors</p><h2>James P. Howard, II<br />+ John F. Beyers</h2></div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The book grew from experience rather than technological evangelism. The central question is not whether online learning is good or bad. That is not a particularly useful question. The useful questions are narrower: what works, for whom, under what conditions, and how do we know?</p>
          <p>Across both editions, contributors address those questions through course design, assessment, student interaction, mathematics anxiety, technological tools, teacher preparation, and the ordinary mechanics of explaining difficult ideas at a distance.</p>
          <p><a href="https://jameshoward.us/books/teaching-learning-mathematics-online/">James Howard’s book page ↗</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
